🎹 Chaos Piano: Phase 1 - Proximity Control Logic

📜 Project Overview

The Chaos Piano is an interactive, multi-sensory art installation designed for the Fondation du Doute. It transforms physical movement into an orchestration of light, sound, and vibration. This repository documents the evolution of the system from a bench prototype to a full-scale industrial control-command architecture.

The "Chaos" Logic
The system functions as an open-loop control-command system. Using an ultrasonic sensor, it monitors the distance of visitors. As a visitor approaches, the system increases the intensity and frequency of the feedback (LED strobing and auditory signals), creating a "chaotic" response that scales with proximity.


🛠 Current Technical State (Phase 1)
The project is currently in the Proof of Concept (PoC) stage, focusing on high-power switching and sensor data filtration.
1. Hardware Stack
Component               Industrial Role                 Specification
Microcontroller         Logic Unit (PLC equivalent)     Arduino Uno R3 (Atmega328P)
Sensor                  Data Acquisition                HC-SR04 (Ultrasonic)
Actuator                Power Output                    12V High-Brightness LED Strip
Power Interface         Output Stage                    IRLZ44N MOSFET (Logic Level)
Monitoring              Diagnostics                     Eventek ET580 Digital Multimeter

2. Power Architecture
To handle the 12V load while protecting the 5V microcontroller, the system utilizes a Common Ground architecture.
Logic Level Switching: An IRLZ44N MOSFET is used as a low-side switch, allowing the 5V Arduino pins to control the 12V power rail directly.
Signal Filtration: The code implements a Low-Pass Filter (Software Smoothing) to eliminate ultrasonic "noise" and ensure stable LED transitions.

🚀 Final Project Vision
The finished exhibit will scale this logic across a full-sized piano with the following expanded capabilities:
Haptic Feedback: Three 6V vibrating motors attached to the piano strings to create physical resonance.
Audio Manipulation: Piezoelectric microphones capturing raw string vibrations, processed through the Arduino with glitch, distortion, and flanger effects.
Advanced Processing: Transitioning to an RP2040-Zero (Dual-core ARM) to handle multi-threaded sensor acquisition and real-time audio processing.
Industrial Standards: Implementation of a Watchdog Timer for high availability and Opto-couplers for complete electrical isolation between power and logic stages.

📂 Repository Contents
/Code: Optimized C++ firmware for the proximity-to-strobe logic.
/Hardware: Wiring tables and schematics (Fritzing/Draw.io).
/Docs: Technical datasheets for the IRLZ44N and HC-SR04.

🎓 Academic Context
This project serves as a practical application of the Purdue Model (Levels 0 and 1). It demonstrates competency in:
Physical Interaction (Level 0): Interfacing sensors with a dynamic environment.
Control Logic (Level 1): Programming PWM modulation and interrupt-driven logic.
Hardware Safety: Implementing flyback protection (1N4007 diodes) for inductive motor loads.



